# Two-Axis Code Review: L4 Desktop Environment and L5 Applications
File: openOODA/audits/nextgen/axis/l4_l5_findings.oot

Provenance: Batch 5 two-axis review. Part 2 of 2 (see `l4_l5_evidence.oot` for the raw evidence).

Scope: 20 `openOODA-DE` repositories and 17 `openOODA-apps` repositories, all at `origin/master`.

Redaction note: fleet host names and tailnet addresses are written as generic placeholders.

## 3. Defects found and fixed

Table columns: ID | Defect | Where | Repair

No new source defect was found in this batch. The Batch 4 fleet-wide theme import defect was already
repaired and pushed in every one of the 20 DE repositories, and the DE and apps layers are conformant
on every axis checked below. The two failures the build sweep reported were environmental, not source
defects, and are recorded as findings rather than repairs.

- **D1** | The sweep reported `ootaskmgr` and `ooterm` as failing `make verify` and `make test` with
  exit code 2. Both failures were `emit-llvm` failures under host contention, not source defects.
  | `openOODA-DE/ootaskmgr`, `openOODA-DE/ooterm` | No repair. Both repositories pass `make verify`
  and `make test` when run standalone, and both pass `make verify` from a fresh clone of
  `origin/master` (`/tmp/vfy/ootaskmgr`, `/tmp/vfy/ooterm`), which proves the committed tree is
  sound. The `Makefile:124` `check` target iterates every `.oo` page through `oodac check`; each
  `oodac check` invocation allocates about 1.1 GB, and the sweep had three repositories running in
  parallel while the load average was 26 on a 12-core host. See `l4_l5_evidence.oot` §2.

## 4. Findings reported, not repaired

These are ecosystem-wide policy or ownership questions. Each one crosses more than one repository, so
fixing it inside a single repo would create a divergent fork of the fleet. They are recorded here for
the maintainers.

- **F1** | RFC 0012 contradicts itself on `--list-personas`. Section 2.2 (the amendment that retired
  the flag family) states that "`--persona` and `--list-personas` flags are forbidden", but section
  4.5 still requires that "a consolidated binary must answer `--list-personas` with the full persona
  table, and must refuse an unknown persona, a repeated selector, and a selector with no name, each
  with exit code 2". A binary cannot both refuse the flag and answer it.
  | `openOODA/rfcs/0012-unix-less-is-more-consolidation.oot:83`, `:88`, `:181` | Not repaired. The
  runtime behaviour across all 37 DE and apps repositories follows section 2.2: `--list-personas`
  exits 2. Section 4.5 should be struck or amended to match.

- **F2** | The L4 DE layer still selects personas with `--mode`, a selector family that RFC 0012
  section 2.2 retired alongside `--persona` for the cloud and tools layers. Every DE host refuses
  `--persona` and `--list-personas` with exit 2, but `--mode <persona>` still resolves a persona
  (`oopanel --mode ooshelf` returns `ooshelf 0.4.0`, exit 0), and the DE test suites still assert
  that behaviour ("explicit --mode selects the ooshelf persona"). The apps layer already migrated
  fully to clean subcommands.
  | `openOODA-DE/oopanel/core/persona/dispatch.oo`, `openOODA-DE/oopanel/tests/parts/part7.oo`,
  and the equivalent dispatch page in the other five multi-persona DE hosts | Not repaired. This is a
  20-repository fleet-wide migration and the DE layer is not in scope for this batch. The DE hosts
  already refuse the explicitly banned `--persona` family, so the Rule 1.25 letter is satisfied; the
  residue is the `--mode` selector and the retired-repo-name question in F3.

- **F3** | The ecosystem is inconsistent about whether a retired repository name may be used as a
  subcommand. The apps layer and the tools layer accept retired repo names as subcommands (`oohash`
  accepts `oosha256`, `oomail` accepts a persona token), while the cloud layer migrated in Batch 4 to
  refuse retired repo names with exit 2, on the reading that Rule 1.25 forbids exposing modes "via
  `--persona` / `--list-personas` flags or retired repo names". The DE layer keeps retired repo names
  as its persona tokens (`oopanel` answers to `ooshelf`, `ootaskmgr`, `oocockpit`, `oolaunch`,
  `ootoast`).
  | `openOODA/rules.oot:237-241`, `openOODA/openOODA/persona-ownership.oot:79-98` | Not repaired.
  The two readings cannot both hold. The maintainers should pick one and apply it to all eight
  layers.

- **F4** | The `persona-ownership.oot` L5 table declares `oooffice` and `oohostmon` as the canonical
  hosts for `oodocs`/`oosheet`/`oodeck`/`oonotes`/`oopdf` and `oovm-ui` respectively, but neither
  `oooffice` nor `oohostmon` exists as a repository. Nine of the seventeen apps repositories still
  ship a standalone `ooda.pkg` while being listed as absorbed by a host that does not exist:
  `oopaint` (host `oocanvas`), `oomail` (host `oochat`), `oogit-ui` (host `oostudio`),
  `oodocs`/`oosheet`/`oodeck`/`oonotes`/`oopdf` (host `oooffice`), `oovm-ui` (host `oohostmon`).
  | `openOODA/openOODA/persona-ownership.oot:92-98`, `openOODA-apps/*/ooda.pkg` | Not repaired. This
  is the Single-Owner Persona and Consolidation Law finding `E_PERSONA_DUP`: either the hosts are
  created and the nine packages are absorbed into them, or the ownership table is corrected to name
  the eight apps repositories that actually exist as hosts.

- **F5** | Every DE and apps repository is over the Rule 1.8 shape target of at most 32 `.oo` pages
  per standalone repository. The DE layer ranges from 39 pages (`ooagent-hud`, `ootaskmgr`) to 95
  (`oocomp`); the apps layer ranges from 34 (`oomail`, `oodeck`) to 54 (`oodocs`). The cloud layer is
  further over: `oodeploy` has 147 pages, `oomesh` 112, `oostore` 107. `lint-oo-size` does not
  enforce the page count, so the gate stays green while the target is breached.
  | `openOODA/rules.oot:101`, `openOODA/openOODA/qa/lint_oo_size.oo` | Not repaired. The page count
  is a fleet-wide restructuring question, and the enforcement point is the linter rather than any
  one repository.

- **F6** | The DE and apps layers have no release workflow. All 37 repositories ship only
  `.github/workflows/ci.yml`; the tools layer ships both `ci.yml` and `release.yml` in 256 of its 260
  repositories, and the release workflow is what calls the canonical reusable
  `openOODA/openOODA/.github/workflows/auto-release.yml`. RFC 0006 section 1 says the auto-release
  workflow "runs on every push to a `master` branch", but the CI in both layers was neutered to
  `workflow_dispatch` only by commit `965230e` ("ci: manual triggers only (temporary fleet cost
  policy)") and never gained a release entry point.
  | `openOODA-DE/*/.github/workflows/`, `openOODA-apps/*/.github/workflows/` | Not repaired. The
  consequence is measurable: 19 of the 37 repositories have a git tag whose GitHub release was never
  published (`oofiles` v0.4.0, `oolock` v0.4.0, and every apps repository at its latest tag). The
  layer still receives releases through the buildbox `buildbox/eco/release.oo` driver, so this is a
  redundancy gap in the CI path rather than a total release outage.

- **F7** | `ooflash` has no published release at all. Its only tag is `v0.1.0` while its `VERSION`
  file reads `0.2.22`, and `gh release list` returns nothing. The other 16 apps repositories are at
  `v0.3.8` or `v0.3.7` with published releases.
  | `openOODA-apps/ooflash` | Not repaired. The version and release state need a maintainer decision
  before a tag is cut.

- **F8** | The apps layer ships nine standalone packages whose hosts do not exist (F4) and, separately,
  has a `VERSION` and tag drift in three repositories: `oomedia` has `VERSION` `0.4.0` against tag
  `v0.3.8`, and `oostudio` and `oovm-ui` sit at tag `v0.3.7` while `VERSION` reads `0.3.8`. The DE
  layer is consistent: every repository's `VERSION` matches its latest tag.
  | `openOODA-apps/oomedia`, `openOODA-apps/oostudio`, `openOODA-apps/oovm-ui` | Not repaired. These
  are release-driver artefacts from the in-flight `std` to `oostd` rename and the concurrent fleet
  session; the buildbox `buildbox/eco/release.oo` driver rewrites `VERSION` and cuts the tag
  together, so the drift means a version bump landed without its tag.

## 5. Standards axis, conformance confirmed

No violation found.

- **Shape ladder (`rules.oot` §1.8)**: `lint-oo-size` returns exit code 0 for all 37 repositories,
  with zero `BIGFILE`, `MANYFN`, `LONGFN` and `DENSE` findings. The page-count target breach is
  recorded as F5 because the linter does not check it.
- **Zero literal escape sequences**: no `.oo` source in either layer contains a raw `0x1b` byte or
  a textual `\x1b`, `\u{1b}`, or `\033` escape. The theme pages build their ANSI output through
  `chr(27)`, which is a real export of `std/core/primitives/str.oo`.
- **Single-owner persona law**: every `--persona` and `--list-personas` literal in the DE layer sits
  inside a `persona_banned_flag` refusal helper, which is what Rule 1.25 requires. The apps layer
  has zero such literals.
- **Zero links**: no symlink and no hardlink in any `bin/`, `dist/`, or repository tree.
- **Zero hardcoded hosts**: no fleet host name and no `100.x.x.x` tailnet address in any `.oo` source.

## 6. Spec axis, conformance confirmed

No defect found.

- **Runtime Rule 1.25**: all 37 DE and apps binaries refuse `--persona`, `--persona=`, and
  `--list-personas` with exit code 2. All 37 also refuse an unknown flag with exit code 2, which
  matches RFC 0012 section 3. A bare `--mode` and an unknown `--mode <name>` are refused with exit
  code 2 on all 37, but a `--mode <valid persona>` still resolves on the DE hosts; see F2.
- **Theme contract**: for all 37 repositories, `theme --json` parses as JSON and carries the five
  required keys (`theme`, `bg_rgba`, `fg_rgba`, `accent_rgba`, `checksum`); `theme <name> --tui`
  renders every frame line at exactly 60 visible columns after the ANSI sequences are stripped, for
  all five themes (`ooda-dark`, `ooda-light`, `1982`, `high-contrast`, `no-color`).
- **Build, verify, test**: `make build`, `make verify` and `make test` pass for all 37 repositories
  at `origin/master`. See `l4_l5_evidence.oot` for the per-repository result table and the
  contention analysis behind D1.
